menu "Pocket VM (L0 measurement probes)"

    config POCKET_VM_PROBE
        bool "Enable L0 VM probes (docs/quickjs-freertos-vm-spec.md sec.5)"
        default n
        help
            Off by default and meant to stay that way for every normal build.
            On, this compiles in:
              - main/pocket/vmprobe.c, which samples the guest's JS memory
                usage, free heap, largest free block and ui_task's C stack
                high-water mark every 8th frame (frame time and job counts
                every frame) and logs a rate-limited
                "VMPROBE ..." summary (min/median/max over a ~1s window)
                instead of one line per frame.
              - a #ifdef-guarded probe block inside the vendored
                components/quickjs-ng/quickjs-ng/quickjs.c that exposes
                sizeof(JSStackFrame)/sizeof(JSVarRef) (private to that file)
                and a job-queue depth counter around JS_EnqueueJob /
                JS_ExecutePendingJob.
            The six USB-only L0 workloads that used to live under apps/vmprobe/
            (letters A-F) were retired once L0 finished (tag vm-L0);
            `git checkout vm-L0 -- apps/vmprobe tools/vm_l0_capture.py`
            brings them back for a re-measurement. With this on, the probe
            above now samples whatever app is actually running.
            Build a separate build_* directory with this on -- and give that
            directory its OWN sdkconfig file (-D SDKCONFIG=<dir>/sdkconfig;
            see sdkconfig.vmprobe.defaults), because ESP-IDF's default
            sdkconfig path is the project root, shared by every build_*
            directory regardless of -B. Never turn this on in
            sdkconfig.defaults or in the shared root sdkconfig.

    config POCKET_VM_SCHED
        bool "Job-boundary scheduling and wake (docs/vm-L1-design.md, L1)"
        default y
        help
            The L1 execution control. On, a turn's Promise job drain may stop
            BETWEEN two jobs once the turn's time budget is spent, and the rest
            of that drain runs at the top of the next turn before any host
            event or frame() reaches JavaScript; a completion posted from
            another task can also cut short the frame-cap sleep instead of
            waiting out the whole period.

            Off, every knob it adds is set to "unlimited": the drain cannot
            yield, so no continuation turn can exist, the unhandled-rejection
            report point is the one it always was, and the frame cap is the old
            unconditional vTaskDelay. That is the pre-L1 behaviour exactly,
            which is what spec sec.12 means by every stage being revertible --
            if a board run finds something this level broke, this switch puts
            it back without reverting the code.

            Measure the cost with tools/memlog.py rather than assuming it.

    config POCKET_VM_SEGFRAMES
        bool "JS frames in runtime-owned segments, not on the C stack (L2a)"
        default y
        help
            docs/quickjs-freertos-vm-spec.md sec.7 L2a, docs/vm-L2-design.md.
            On, JS_CallInternal takes its JSStackFrame and the block that
            used to be alloca()'d (arguments, locals, operand stack, var_refs)
            from a bump-allocated segment chain the runtime owns
            (components/quickjs-ng/quickjs-ng/quickjs-vmstack.h). Segments come
            from the guest heap through js_malloc_rt, so they count against
            JS_SetMemoryLimit; one 4 KiB segment stays resident from the first
            call to JS_FreeRuntime, a frame that does not fit chains another,
            and a frame larger than a segment gets one of its own.

            What this changes for an app: per JS call the ui task's 20 KiB C
            stack loses the frame (48 B) and the locals block, keeping only
            JS_CallInternal's own C frame -- C recursion itself is still
            there (that is L2b), so deep recursion still ends in the same
            RangeError from JS_SetMaxStackSize; a frame the heap refuses is
            an InternalError "out of memory", the error a generator's heap
            frame already produced. Generator / async frames are untouched.

            JS_SetMaxStackSize (app_session.c: 20 KiB) is read twice on this
            path (design D10): as the C-stack limit it always was, and as a
            byte budget for the segments -- the sum of every live frame, in
            bytes, not a frame count. Whichever fills first raises the
            RangeError; the budget is tested before the heap is asked, so
            "too deep" stays RangeError and only a frame the heap cannot
            hold is InternalError. The budget is the guard that survives
            L2b's removal of the C recursion. tools/vmtest/budget_probe.sh
            shows it working with the C-stack guard lifted.

            Off, JS_CallInternal is the upstream alloca path and JSRuntime
            has no segment state: the pre-L2a behaviour exactly, which is
            what spec sec.12 means by every stage being revertible. The host
            harness builds the same switch as tools/vmtest/build.sh
            "-alloca" variants.

    config POCKET_VM_FAIR
        bool "Fair ordering: run the pumps on a continuation turn too"
        default n
        help
            OFF (the shipping default) is COMPAT ORDERING, the rule
            docs/vm-L1-design.md sec.2.1 states: a turn that ended with jobs
            queued finishes them at the top of the next turn, and no host event
            reaches JavaScript until the queue is empty. One logical drain then
            has exactly the job order the pre-L1 single drain produced, and the
            only thing that changed is that wall clock passes between two of
            its jobs. This switch compiles to nothing when it is off.

            ON, a continuation turn that STILL has jobs left runs the same
            pumps an ordinary turn runs -- everything except frame() and the
            exit() check -- after its drain has yielded. A completion that a
            radio, a bus or a timer recorded during the drain is settled there
            and its reaction is enqueued at the TAIL of the queue, behind the
            jobs of the unfinished drain, so FIFO inside the queue is exactly
            what it was; what changes is only that a host event can reach
            JavaScript between two jobs of one logical drain.

            The cost compat ordering pays is completion latency on an app whose
            drain needs continuations -- measured (device) at ~1.9x the pre-L1
            path under audio contention (docs/vm-L1-report.md sec.6). This mode
            exists so that tradeoff can be measured rather than argued, and so
            L2 can revisit it with evidence. Two things are deliberately NOT
            made fair, because neither can be made safe at a job boundary:
            exit() (delivered as an uncatchable interrupt, it would cut the
            drain it lands in) and frame() (it would put a frame inside a
            chain). See docs/vm-L1-report.md sec.10.

    config POCKET_UI_TASK_CORE
        int "Core the ui task is pinned to (-1 = let FreeRTOS choose)"
        range -1 1
        default 1
        help
            main/main.c's ui_task owns the whole UI, every screen and the JS
            session (docs/vm-L1-design.md sec.4.1), so where it runs decides
            where every JS job runs. -1 creates it with tskNO_AFFINITY, which
            is what xTaskCreate() did here before; 0 or 1 pins it.

            The default is 1 because that is what the board measured
            (docs/vm-L1-report.md sec.8, all 実測(device)): core 1 is
            indistinguishable from no affinity -- the JS turn medians match to
            0.1%, the LCD transfer to 0.04%, DIRAM to the byte -- while core 0
            costs 5.6% of a compute-bound turn, 7.6% of the transfer, and 16%
            of an HTTP request running on the other core, because core 0 is
            where ESP-IDF fixes the Wi-Fi driver task. Pinning also removes the
            one migration the board does take (measured: 1 in 1,800 idle
            frames, docs/vm-l1-clock.md sec.2), which is what makes a per-core
            clock (CONFIG_POCKET_VM_CCOUNT) possible at all.

            The three values are the SAME binary with one immediate changed --
            xTaskCreatePinnedToCore() is the only call site either way -- so a
            measurement across them is not confounded by a code-path change.

    config POCKET_VM_CCOUNT
        bool "Read CCOUNT, not esp_timer, for the L1 job budget"
        default n
        help
            The L1 turn budget asks the clock how much of its 8 ms is left,
            once every VM_JOB_STRIDE jobs (vm_sched.h). CCOUNT --
            the per-core cycle counter, RSR CCOUNT, the one main/scene/flower.c
            already uses -- costs 25 ns a read against esp_timer_get_time()'s
            833 ns (measured (device), docs/vm-l1-clock.md sec.1).

            It is per core, so it is only sound when the task that runs the
            drain cannot migrate: components/pocketjs_guest/src/vm_clock.c
            refuses to compile unless CONFIG_POCKET_UI_TASK_CORE >= 0.

            Whether it is worth having is a measurement, not a preference --
            docs/vm-L1-report.md sec.8 records what each clock costs on the
            job-heavy workloads.

    config POCKET_VM_L1_CLOCKBENCH
        bool "Enable L1 clock-cost bench (docs/vm-l1-clock.md)"
        default n
        help
            A throwaway bench for one question: what does reading the clock
            cost, and does the ui task migrate cores? NOT part of any normal
            build. On, this compiles in:
              - a boot-time loop (main.c) that times esp_cpu_get_cycle_count()
                (RSR CCOUNT) against esp_timer_get_time() (systimer), logging
                "CLOCKBENCH ..." lines once, then falls through to the normal
                boot.
              - a per-frame xPortGetCoreID() migration counter in ui_task,
                logging a rate-limited "BENCH_CORE ..." summary.
            Same isolation rule as POCKET_VM_PROBE: give the build directory
            its own -D SDKCONFIG=<dir>/sdkconfig, never enable this in the
            shared root sdkconfig or in sdkconfig.defaults. See
            sdkconfig.vmclockbench.defaults.

endmenu
